vmforai phone [--new] [--lan|--tailscale] [--port N] [--json]
vmforai phone off
vmforai phone ssh NAME KEY|FILE|off
vmforai phone notify [off|test] [--server URL] [--token TOKEN] [--status] [--json]

Your VMs on the phone. "vmforai phone" starts a small web server on this
computer and shows a QR code: scan it with the phone's camera, and its
browser lists the VMs; tap one for its agent (Claude Code, or the one you
chose for it) or a shell, in a terminal on the phone. It is the same session
as on the computer (tmux on the VM): start a task here, look at it from the
couch. A VM that is off is started. A bar on the phone gives Esc, Tab,
Shift+Tab, Ctrl, the arrows; the box below it sends a whole message at once
(the phone's own keyboard and dictation work there). A key from the bar goes
after what was typed: type the start of a name, tap Tab, it is completed.

Where the phone reaches it: over Tailscale (default; tailscale.com, free):
encrypted, from anywhere, and nothing opens to the internet. Install it on
this computer (curl -fsSL https://tailscale.com/install.sh | sh; sudo
tailscale up) and the app on the phone, with the same account. --lan: on the
local network instead (the phone on the same Wi-Fi; not encrypted, and
networks of schools or hotels often keep devices apart). The choice and
--port (default 7700) are remembered.

The link (in the QR code) holds a random key; without it the server answers
nothing. --new makes a new one: the old link stops working. It runs until
"vmforai phone off" or until this computer restarts. Log of who connected:
~/.cache/vmforai/phone.log. The page's terminal (xterm.js) is fetched once
from cdn.jsdelivr.net (checked against its hash) and then served from here.

Tap a VM's name on the page for more: a task for it ("vmforai run") with
its output followed live, the earlier tasks, stopping one; fixing a GitHub
issue of its project ("vmforai fix"); starting and shutting it down. Added
to the home screen, the page opens like an app.

SSH apps (Termius, Blink, JuiceSSH, ...): "phone ssh NAME KEY" puts the app's
public key on the VM and gives the VM a port here (7701, 7702, ...) that
leads to its SSH server, starting the VM when needed (connect again if the
app gives up while it boots). With autostart on, Claude Code opens on login.
"phone ssh NAME off" takes the key off the VM again.

Notifications on the phone: "phone notify" sends one when a task or a fix
ends, when an agent waits for you (a permission, a question) and when a new
VM is ready, through ntfy (ntfy.sh, free, no account; or --server, a server
of your own, with --token when it wants one). It makes a topic with a random
name, which works like a password, and shows a code for subscribing to it in
the ntfy app on the phone (Play Store, F-Droid, App Store); tapping a
notification opens that VM on the phone page. What they say (VM names, a
task's first line) passes through that server. "phone notify test" sends a
test, "off" stops them (on again: a new topic). The page's list has the same
switch. "vmforai notify NAME off" leaves one VM out, here and on the phone.

--json: the address, link, QR code rows and SSH ports, for scripts and the UI.

Examples:
  vmforai phone
  vmforai phone --lan
  vmforai phone ssh dev "ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3Nza... termius"
  vmforai phone ssh dev ~/Downloads/termius.pub
  vmforai phone off
EOF
