    run) cat <<EOF
vmforai run NAME [--claude|--codex|--gemini|--opencode|--aider] [--wait] [--] TASK...

Gives an agent a task on the VM and comes back: the agent works alone, in a
tmux session of its own (run-ID), so it goes on when this computer
disconnects or sleeps. Nobody is there to answer its questions, so it acts
without asking (as with "vmforai yolo"); what it does stays in the VM, and
"vmforai undo NAME" takes it back. It starts in the VM's folder (DIR, or the
project's: "vmforai help project"). The task can also come on stdin.

When it ends a desktop notification appears here; with --wait you follow
its output here instead. A libvirt VM that was off is started, and shut down
again after the task (unless something else uses it). While a task runs the
VM counts as used: autostop and the idle shutdown leave it on (for the idle
shutdown of an older VM, run "vmforai idle NAME MINUTES" again once).

Claude Code runs with -p (its steps are shown one per line), Codex with exec,
Gemini CLI with -p, OpenCode with run --auto, aider with --message.

Windows VMs (Claude Code only): no tmux there; the task runs in a Windows
process of its own (started through WMI), which goes on as well when the
connection ends. "vmforai runs" shows, follows and stops it the same way.

Examples:
  vmforai run dev "fix the failing tests"
  vmforai run dev --codex --wait -- "add a --verbose flag"
  vmforai run dev < task.md
  vmforai changes dev                      what it changed in the project
EOF
