    net) cat <<EOF
vmforai net NAME [restricted | open | allow HOST... | deny HOST... | blocked]

With a restricted network the VM reaches only the hosts on its list: the
agents' services, package registries and GitHub at first. Then an agent
that acts without asking (yolo, "vmforai run") cannot send your code or
keys anywhere else, or fetch from anywhere else.

It is enforced on this computer, where the VM cannot change it: a libvirt
network filter (nwfilter "$NET_FILTER") on the VM's card lets only SSH,
DHCP, DNS and a proxy on this computer through, and the proxy lets HTTP and
HTTPS through to the hosts on the list (other ports: HOST:PORT). On the VM,
proxy settings make the agents, git, npm, pip and apt/pacman use it (the
system-wide ones need sudo there). Agents already running keep their old
settings until they are started again. The VM's libvirt network and other
VMs are not changed. The proxy needs python3 here, and runs while the VM does.

Without arguments it shows the mode, the list and what was refused lately
(blocked: more). The list is a file you can edit too:
${CONF_DIR/#$HOME/\~}/net/NAME.allow (a host, *.domain, HOST:PORT per line).
Libvirt VMs on a libvirt network only, Linux.

Examples:
  vmforai net dev restricted
  vmforai net dev allow api.example.com *.internal.test
  vmforai net dev                          what was refused: allow it if it was needed
  vmforai net dev open
EOF
      ;;
    try) cat <<EOF
vmforai try NAME N [--agents LIST] [--memory MB] [--stop] [--] [TASK...]
vmforai try NAME --clean

Makes N copies of the VM (NAME-t1, NAME-t2, ...) and gives each the same
task, so you can pick the best result. The copies take seconds: libvirt
clones the VM's disk, which shares the downloaded base image, so only what
changed in the VM is copied (its snapshots are not). Each copy gets a new
name, UUID and MAC address, its own profile and the project's state.

--agents lets different agents take turns (claude,codex,gemini: one each);
by default all use the VM's agent. Each copy does the task like "vmforai
run" (alone, without asking) and shuts down when it is done; a notification
comes for each. The copies run at the same time: N times the VM's memory has
to be free here (--memory MB gives each less). The VM itself has to be off,
so its disk holds still: --stop shuts it down first. Without a task the
copies are simply yours to use.

Then compare ("vmforai tries NAME", "vmforai changes NAME-t2"), take the
best as a branch ("vmforai take NAME-t2"), and delete them all with
"vmforai try NAME --clean" (the branches you took stay). Copies work only
from VMs made with "vmforai create" (libvirt, no UEFI).

Examples:
  vmforai try dev 3 "make the start page load faster"
  vmforai try dev 2 --agents claude,codex --memory 2048 -- "fix issue 42"
  vmforai try dev --clean
EOF
