    fix) cat <<EOF
vmforai fix NAME ISSUE [--test CMD] [--tries N] [--branch BRANCH] [--no-pr] [--draft]
                        [--yes] [--discard] [--codex|--gemini|--opencode|--aider]

From a GitHub issue to a pull request, while you do something else. In the
VM's project ("vmforai project"):
  1. reads the issue (number or URL) and its comments with the GitHub CLI (gh)
  2. sends the project again, as it is checked out here, a fresh copy
  3. gives the issue to the agent as a task ("vmforai run"), to fix and commit
  4. runs the tests on the VM: --test CMD (kept for next time; "make check",
     "npm test", "pytest"...). When they fail, the agent gets their output
     and tries again, --tries times in all (default 2)
  5. brings the work back as the branch vmforai/issue-ISSUE (or BRANCH): the
     agent's commits (what it did not commit only when it committed nothing)
  6. when the tests pass: pushes it to origin and opens a pull request,
     "Fixes #ISSUE", against the branch that was sent (--draft: as a draft)

Without a test command the agent runs the tests it finds, and the pull
request says that vmforai did not check. --no-pr stops at the branch.
Only this computer talks to GitHub; the VM gets the issue's text.

The agent follows the issue's text without asking. When people other than
you wrote some of it, fix says so and asks first (--yes: do not ask). The VM
has no access to your GitHub, and what it makes is a pull request for you
to look at, not a merge.

A VM that was off is started, and shut down again at the end. Work on the
VM that was not taken back stops it (--discard: set it aside on the VM).

Examples:
  vmforai fix dev 42 --test "npm test"
  vmforai fix dev https://github.com/me/app/issues/42 --codex --draft
EOF
